Outlaw FieldKit License, version 1.1

Copyright (c) 2026 Sup095

This is a source-available license. It is NOT an open-source license:
it lets you use, run and improve the software, and charge people for
a server you run with it, but not sell the software itself or publish
copies of it elsewhere. Everything not allowed below is reserved by
the licensor.

What changed from version 1.0: running a server for other people may
now earn money (section 2(f)), to pay for the servers and to make a
reasonable profit. Selling or handing out the software itself is still
not allowed.


1. Words used here

"The software" means the code, documentation and release files in
the official repository, https://github.com/Sup095/Outlaw-Fieldkit,
and any copy or changed version of them.

"The licensor" means Sup095, the copyright holder.

"You" means the person or organisation using the software under this
license.

"Hosting income" means money you take for a server you run with the
software: access fees, subscriptions, donations, sponsorships, and
ads shown around the service (not inserted into the pages people
visit through it).

"Commercial use" means making money from the software itself, or using
it for money in any way other than hosting income. That includes:
selling or licensing the software or a changed version; charging for
its code or its release files; putting it, or a rebranded copy of it,
into a product you sell or license to others; and selling it to
businesses to run as their own (white-labelling).


2. What you may do

As long as you follow this license and do not make commercial use of
the software, you may:

  (a) Use it. Download it, run it, and use every part of it. That
      includes running your own relay and run server, for yourself and
      for the people you choose to let in, and using a server that
      someone else runs.

  (b) Change it for yourself. Make changes and run the changed version
      on your own computers and servers, and let the people you choose
      use your server while it runs that version.

  (c) Contribute. Fork the official repository on GitHub, make changes
      in that fork, and offer them back as pull requests. Your fork
      must stay a GitHub fork of the official repository.

  (d) Pass on a release file. Give an unchanged official release file
      (for example OUTPOST-STANDALONE.html) to someone, privately and
      free of charge, for their own use under this license.

  (e) Point people to it. Link to the official repository, its
      releases, or a server running the software.

  (f) Earn hosting income. Run a server with the software, or a
      changed version of it as allowed in 2(b), for other people, and
      take hosting income for it: to pay for the servers, the
      connection and your time, and to make a reasonable profit. If
      you do:
        - say, where people pay or sign up, that the service is built
          on Outlaw FieldKit, with a link to the official repository;
        - be honest about what people get, and keep to the laws that
          apply to you, including consumer, privacy and tax law; and
        - keep to the licenses of the parts that are other people's
          work (section 5). Some of them, such as libcurl-transport
          (AGPL-3.0) in the lighter browser, ask that people who use
          them over a network can get their source; the software
          already links it, so do not remove those links.
      The software's own files, the official release files included,
      stay free of charge: you charge for the service, not for them.


3. What you may not do

  (a) Make commercial use of the software.

  (b) Redistribute it. Do not publish, upload, mirror or hand out the
      software, a changed version of it, or a substantial part of it,
      anywhere other than as allowed in 2(c) and 2(d). That covers
      other Git repositories (on GitHub or anywhere else), other code
      hosts, package registries, app and extension stores, download and
      file-sharing sites, and bundles with other software. Running the
      software on your server so people can use it is not
      redistribution; handing out its files is.

  (c) Remove or change the copyright notice, this license, or the
      credit to the licensor in the software.

  (d) Present a server you run, or a changed version you make, as the
      official one, or as made by the licensor.

  (e) Sublicense the software, or grant anyone more rights in it than
      this license gives you.


4. Your contributions

When you send a pull request, a patch or any other contribution to
the official repository:

  (a) you confirm that you wrote it, or otherwise have the right to
      give it under these terms;

  (b) you keep the copyright in it; and

  (c) you allow the licensor to use, copy, change and publish it as
      part of the software under this license, and under any other
      terms the licensor chooses for later versions of the software.


5. Other people's work

Some parts of the software, and some things it downloads or runs, are
other people's work under their own licenses, and those licenses
apply to them instead of this one. This license does not limit
anything their licenses allow. They include:

  - vendor/web-llm.js, a build of WebLLM (MLC AI), under the Apache
    License 2.0;
  - the lighter browser's client files, fetched when its image is
    built: Scramjet and bare-mux (MIT), libcurl-transport (AGPL-3.0)
    and libcurl.js (LGPL-3.0);
  - programs fetched or installed when the relay or run server is set
    up or runs, such as Chromium, FFmpeg, PulseAudio, Wine, cloudflared
    and Tailscale;
  - AI models you download to use with the software.


6. If you break this license

If you break any of these terms, your rights under this license end.
If it is the first time, and you stop within 30 days of the licensor
telling you about it, your rights come back as if they had never
ended.


7. No warranty, no liability

The software is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind,
express or implied, including the warranties of merchantability,
fitness for a particular purpose and non-infringement. To the extent
the law allows, the licensor is not liable for any claim, damages or
other liability arising from the software or from its use, whether
in contract, tort or otherwise.


8. Asking for more

If you want to do something this license does not allow, ask the
licensor by opening an issue in the official repository. Only
permission given in writing by the licensor counts.
